spans.s:
Yajña-gāthā denotes a verse (Gāthā) containing a maxim as
to the sacrifice of any kind or sort,^1 or, as it is expressed in the
Mahābhārata,^2 a ‘verse sung regarding the sacrifice’ (gāthā
yajña-gītā).
1) Aitareya Brāhmaṇa, iii. 43, 5;
Āśvalāyana Śrauta Sūtra, ii. 12, 6;
Śānkhāyana Śrauta Sūtra, xvi. 8, 26;
9, 6, etc.
2) xii. 791. 2316.

Yājyā (scil. ṛc, ‘verse’) denotes the words uttered at the
moment of offering the sacrifice, ‘consecrating sacrificial
formula,’ in the later Saṃhitās^1 and the Brāhmaṇas.^2
1) Taittirīya Saṃhitā, i. 5, 2, 1;
6, 10, 5; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 20;
xx. 12, etc.
2) Aitareya Brāhmaṇa, i. 4, 8; 11, 10;
ii. 13, 2; 26, 3. 5. 6; 40, 8; iii. 32, 1;
Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa, i. 4, 2, 19; iii. 4,
4, 2; vii. 2, 7, 11, etc.
